# Exception coverage reference review

This is a local parser and coverage review for the two retained ARM64 candidate
images. It does not execute unwinding, establish a native stack/cost bound, or
transfer the old binaries' scope to the changed checkout. Exact reference bytes
and origins are retained in [references.json](references.json). The installed
Rust documentation identifies Rust 1.93.1; the tagged LLVM sources are format
and algorithm references, not authentication of the installed macOS unwinder
or the linker that produced these images.

## Personality rules

The installed [Rust personality source](rust-1.93.1-gcc.rs.html) agrees with the
[tagged implementation](https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/blob/1.93.1/library/std/src/sys/personality/gcc.rs).
For an ordinary return address, the personality searches using one byte less;
when `ip_before_instr` is nonzero it uses the supplied IP unchanged. Thus an
ARM64 `BL` at `P`, whose return address is `P+4`, selects byte `P+3`. Do not
subtract again after normalizing. Search and cleanup phases differ: a cleanup
is not a search-phase handler; a catch/filter can stop the search. On ARM64 the
installed landing context supplies the exception in X0 and zero in X1. These
facts do not determine every restored register or SP. The forced-unwind filter
special case must not be generalized into an invented rule for every action.

The installed [LSDA parser](rust-1.93.1-eh.rs.html) agrees with
[Rust 1.93.1](https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/blob/1.93.1/library/std/src/sys/personality/dwarf/eh.rs).
Call-site intervals use `func_start`, including when an explicit LPStart is
present. Landing offsets use LPStart, defaulting to `func_start`. Ranges are
half-open. Null LSDA and a matched zero landing offset mean no action; an IP
missing from a present call-site table means termination. Nonzero action
indices are one-based offsets from the action-table start. Rust classifies
only the first SLEB type index: zero means cleanup, positive means catch,
negative means filter. It does not follow the generic C++ action chain or
resolve exception type matches. Structural chain data must remain separate
from that classification. Unsupported pointer encodings, malformed offsets,
overflow, truncated LEBs and ambiguous table spans must refuse or stay unknown.
A PC-relative encoded pointer is relative to its own encoded location. Limiting
the first implementation to omitted LPStart, observed call-site encoding and
known type-table headers is a sound fail-closed scope restriction.

## Compact table rules

The [local SDK header](MacOSX26.5-compact_unwind_encoding.h) defines the regular
and compressed records. Regular entry offsets are image-relative. Compressed
low 24-bit offsets are relative to the first-level page's function offset;
the high eight bits choose either the common encoding table or the local
table after subtracting the common count. The next entry or next-page boundary
closes the interval. The final first-level item is a sentinel, not another
second-level page. Bounds, ordering, counts and every indirection need checks.
ARM64 frame, frameless and DWARF modes have different meanings; a DWARF entry's
low bits are an FDE hint, not a stack size. Frame encodings describe unwind
recovery, not total stack allocation at every exceptional entry point.

The [LLVM cursor reference](llvm-21.1.0-UnwindCursor.hpp) looks up an LSDA by an
exact selected compact-start match when `HAS_LSDA` is set. Personality indices
are one-based; the array describes signed image-relative pointer-slot locations,
not direct routine addresses. DWARF fallback can replace region start, LSDA and
personality using FDE/CIE data. Unsupported FDE/CFI remains an explicit frontier.
[LLVM's linker](llvm-21.1.0-MachO-UnwindInfoSection.cpp) can fold adjacent ranges
with equal encoding/personality and no LSDA. Therefore compact starts cannot
replace the function-start inventory. It also omits the compact personality for
DWARF entries: a zero compact index alone cannot prove absence of a handler.
The general header's not-function-start marker must not silently turn an
interior range into a source function; rejecting unimplemented cases is safe.

## Coverage and stack boundary

Our conservative analysis inference is to seed every listed landing pad of
every reached owner, even when ordinary control flow never reaches a protected
call site. This avoids treating the observed BL list as a proof that no other
operation can unwind. It deliberately overapproximates reachability and must
not be reported as an observed exception trace.

Landing-pad entry is an exceptional transfer inside a live function. Starting
it with the ordinary function-entry SP/register abstract state is unjustified;
so is assuming the normal prologue ran exactly once or the normal epilogue has
already run. Required evidence includes the actual unwinder/CFI register
reconstruction and the landing path's own effects. Until then, record unknown
entry stack/register state and retain outgoing cleanup, resume, abort, indirect
and external edges. The unwinder and native callees have separate resource
costs. Finite metadata coverage neither prices those costs nor establishes
termination or a whole-program workspace bound.

## Independent mutation partition

The separate test file will cover exact Rust first-action classification,
one-based action offsets, half-open rows, zero-pad versus missing-row behavior,
truncated/unsupported encodings, out-of-owner ranges and landing addresses,
regular/compressed bases and common/local encoding selection, malformed page
counts/bounds, missing LSDA matches and DWARF markers. Synthetic graph fixtures
should additionally retain unreachable-from-normal-flow landing pads and
explicit exceptional-state frontiers. Root separately checks the whole-body
call-graph universe; the implementer owns parser implementation and actual-image
capture. No runtime build or worker execution is part of this review.
